2. TYPES OF RECURSION
===============================================================================

2.1 DIRECT vs INDIRECT RECURSION
--------------------------------
DIRECT RECURSION: Function calls itself directly
```
int factorial(int n) {
    if (n <= 1) return 1;
    return n * factorial(n-1);  // Direct call to itself
}
```

INDIRECT RECURSION: Function calls itself through other functions
```
int funcA(int n) {
    if (n <= 1) return 1;
    return funcB(n-1);
}

int funcB(int n) {
    return funcA(n-1);  // Calls funcA, creating indirect recursion
}
```

2.2 TAIL vs NON-TAIL RECURSION
------------------------------
TAIL RECURSION: Recursive call is the last operation in the function
```
int factorial_tail(int n, int result) {
    if (n <= 1) return result;
    return factorial_tail(n-1, n * result);  // Last operation
}
```

NON-TAIL RECURSION: Operations are performed after the recursive call
```
int factorial(int n) {
    if (n <= 1) return 1;
    return n * factorial(n-1);  // Multiplication after recursive call
}
```

Advantage of Tail Recursion: Can be optimized by compilers to use constant stack space

2.3 LINEAR vs TREE RECURSION
----------------------------
LINEAR RECURSION: Each function call makes only one recursive call
```
int factorial(int n) {
    if (n <= 1) return 1;
    return n * factorial(n-1);  // Single recursive call
}
```

TREE RECURSION: Each function call makes multiple recursive calls
```
int fibonacci(int n) {
    if (n <= 1) return n;
    return fibonacci(n-1) + fibonacci(n-2);  // Two recursive calls
}
```

Tree recursion creates a tree-like structure of function calls:
                    fib(5)
                   /      \
               fib(4)      fib(3)
              /     \      /     \
          fib(3)  fib(2) fib(2) fib(1)

2.4 NESTED RECURSION
--------------------
Recursive call with a recursive argument
```
int ackermann(int m, int n) {
    if (m == 0) return n + 1;
    if (n == 0) return ackermann(m-1, 1);
    return ackermann(m-1, ackermann(m, n-1));  // Nested recursive calls
}
```

2.5 MUTUAL RECURSION
--------------------
Two or more functions call each other recursively
```
int isEven(int n) {
    if (n == 0) return 1;
    return isOdd(n-1);
}

int isOdd(int n) {
    if (n == 0) return 0;
    return isEven(n-1);
}
```

