3. IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS
===============================================================================

3.1 STEP-BY-STEP DRY RUN
------------------------
Let's trace factorial(4) execution:

Call Stack Growth:
Step 1: factorial(4) called
        Stack: [factorial(4)]
        Check: 4 <= 1? No
        Execute: return 4 * factorial(3)

Step 2: factorial(3) called
        Stack: [factorial(4), factorial(3)]
        Check: 3 <= 1? No
        Execute: return 3 * factorial(2)

Step 3: factorial(2) called
        Stack: [factorial(4), factorial(3), factorial(2)]
        Check: 2 <= 1? No
        Execute: return 2 * factorial(1)

Step 4: factorial(1) called
        Stack: [factorial(4), factorial(3), factorial(2), factorial(1)]
        Check: 1 <= 1? Yes
        Return: 1

Call Stack Unwinding:
Step 5: factorial(1) returns 1
        Stack: [factorial(4), factorial(3), factorial(2)]
        factorial(2) computes: 2 * 1 = 2
        Return: 2

Step 6: factorial(2) returns 2
        Stack: [factorial(4), factorial(3)]
        factorial(3) computes: 3 * 2 = 6
        Return: 6

Step 7: factorial(3) returns 6
        Stack: [factorial(4)]
        factorial(4) computes: 4 * 6 = 24
        Return: 24

Step 8: factorial(4) returns 24
        Stack: []
        Final result: 24

3.2 COMMON MISTAKES BEGINNERS MAKE
----------------------------------
MISTAKE 1: Missing Base Case
```
int infiniteRecursion(int n) {
    return n + infiniteRecursion(n-1);  // No base case!
}
// Result: Stack Overflow Error
```

MISTAKE 2: Incorrect Base Case
```
int factorial(int n) {
    if (n == 1) return 1;  // Wrong! What about n=0?
    return n * factorial(n-1);
}
// factorial(0) will cause infinite recursion
```

MISTAKE 3: Not Moving Toward Base Case
```
int badFunction(int n) {
    if (n == 0) return 0;
    return badFunction(n);  // n doesn't change!
}
// Infinite recursion with same parameter
```

MISTAKE 4: Forgetting to Return Recursive Call Result
```
int factorial(int n) {
    if (n <= 1) return 1;
    n * factorial(n-1);  // Missing return statement!
}
```

MISTAKE 5: Modifying Parameters Incorrectly
```
int sum(int n) {
    if (n == 0) return 0;
    return sum(n--);  // Post-decrement! n doesn't change before call
}
// Should be: return n + sum(n-1);
```

3.3 TIME AND SPACE COMPLEXITY
-----------------------------
TIME COMPLEXITY:
- Depends on number of recursive calls and work done in each call
- Linear Recursion: O(n) if each call does O(1) work
- Tree Recursion: Can be exponential, e.g., O(2^n) for naive Fibonacci

SPACE COMPLEXITY:
- Primarily determined by maximum depth of recursion (call stack)
- Each recursive call adds a frame to call stack
- Linear Recursion: O(n) space for n recursive calls
- Tree Recursion: O(depth) space, not O(total calls)

Examples:
factorial(n): Time O(n), Space O(n)
fibonacci(n): Time O(2^n), Space O(n) - depth is n
binary_search(n): Time O(log n), Space O(log n)

3.4 RECURSION vs DYNAMIC PROGRAMMING
------------------------------------
Problem: Many recursive solutions recalculate the same subproblems

Example: Fibonacci
fibonacci(5) calls fibonacci(3) multiple times:
                    fib(5)
                   /      \
               fib(4)      fib(3)    ← fib(3) calculated
              /     \      /     \
          fib(3)  fib(2) fib(2) fib(1) ← fib(3) calculated again!
         /     \
      fib(2)  fib(1)

MEMOIZATION: Store results of expensive function calls
```
Map<Integer, Integer> memo = new HashMap<>();

int fibonacci(int n) {
    if (n <= 1) return n;
    if (memo.containsKey(n)) return memo.get(n);
    
    int result = fibonacci(n-1) + fibonacci(n-2);
    memo.put(n, result);
    return result;
}
```

Memoization transforms O(2^n) to O(n) time complexity!

