6. BEST PRACTICES & INTUITION BUILDING
===============================================================================

6.1 WHEN TO USE RECURSION
-------------------------
USE RECURSION WHEN:
• Problem has recursive structure (trees, graphs)
• Problem can be broken into similar subproblems
• Backtracking is needed
• Divide and conquer applies
• Mathematical problems with recurrence relations

AVOID RECURSION WHEN:
• Simple iterative solution exists
• Deep recursion might cause stack overflow
• Performance is critical and iterative solution is faster
• Memory usage is a concern

6.2 PROBLEM BREAKDOWN STRATEGY
------------------------------
Step 1: Identify the base case
- What's the simplest version of the problem?
- When should recursion stop?

Step 2: Identify the recursive case
- How can you reduce the problem size?
- What parameters change between calls?

Step 3: Trust the recursion
- Assume recursive calls work correctly
- Focus on combining results

Step 4: Trace through small examples
- Verify base cases work
- Check that recursion progresses toward base case

6.3 RECURSION TREE VISUALIZATION
--------------------------------
For fibonacci(4):
                    fib(4)
                   /      \
               fib(3)      fib(2)
              /     \      /     \
          fib(2)  fib(1) fib(1) fib(0)
         /     \
      fib(1)  fib(0)

Insights:
- Total nodes = total function calls
- Depth = maximum stack size
- Repeated subtrees = repeated calculations

6.4 PATTERN RECOGNITION
-----------------------
Common Recursive Patterns:

1. LINEAR PROCESSING: f(n) = operation + f(n-1)
   Example: factorial, sum, array traversal

2. TREE PROCESSING: f(n) = combine(f(left), f(right))
   Example: binary tree operations, divide and conquer

3. CHOICE/DECISION: f(n) = best(include, exclude)
   Example: subset problems, knapsack

4. GENERATE ALL: f(n) = generate each possibility
   Example: permutations, combinations

5. BACKTRACKING: f(state) = try each option, recurse, undo
   Example: N-Queens, Sudoku, path finding

