8. QUICK REVISION SHEET
===============================================================================

RECURSION FUNDAMENTALS:
----------------------
✓ Function calls itself with modified parameters
✓ Must have base case (stopping condition)
✓ Must have recursive case (problem reduction)
✓ Each call creates new stack frame

BASE CASE CHECKLIST:
-------------------
□ Handles simplest version of problem
□ Returns direct result without recursion
□ Prevents infinite recursion
□ Covers all edge cases (empty, single element, etc.)

RECURSIVE CASE CHECKLIST:
-------------------------
□ Reduces problem size
□ Moves closer to base case
□ Correctly combines sub-problem results
□ Maintains problem invariants

COMMON PATTERNS:
---------------
1. Linear: f(n) = process(n) + f(n-1)
2. Tree: f(n) = combine(f(left), f(right))
3. Backtrack: try → recurse → undo
4. Divide & Conquer: divide → solve → combine

TIME COMPLEXITY RULES:
---------------------
• Linear recursion: O(n) if O(1) work per call
• Tree recursion: O(branches^depth)
• Master theorem for divide & conquer:
  T(n) = aT(n/b) + f(n)

SPACE COMPLEXITY:
----------------
• Always O(recursion depth) for call stack
• Additional space depends on algorithm
• Tail recursion can be optimized to O(1)

OPTIMIZATION TECHNIQUES:
-----------------------
□ Memoization (top-down DP)
□ Tail recursion conversion
□ Iterative conversion when appropriate
□ Early termination conditions

DEBUGGING TIPS:
--------------
□ Trace through small examples manually
□ Print parameters at function entry
□ Verify base cases handle all scenarios
□ Check that recursive calls progress toward base case
□ Use proper indentation to visualize call depth

WHEN TO USE RECURSION:
---------------------
✓ Tree/Graph traversal
✓ Divide and conquer algorithms
✓ Backtracking problems
✓ Mathematical recurrences
✓ Problems with recursive structure

WHEN TO AVOID:
-------------
✗ Simple iterative solution exists
✗ Deep recursion (stack overflow risk)
✗ Performance critical code
✗ Memory constraints

